{
  "image_name": "0b302c02289b65064accea6cb56f44ca.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how RNA structures in the 3′ untranslated region (3′ UTR) control mRNA decay by either promoting degradation or protecting transcripts from it. The top schematic shows that single-stranded regions can expose sequence motifs that recruit proteins or microRNA machinery, including decay-promoting factors as well as stabilizing factors. In contrast, when the RNA folds into stem-loop structures, these same motifs may be hidden by base-pairing, preventing factor binding. Some structures also appear to directly recruit structure-specific decay factors such as nucleases, while other folded regions physically block exonuclease access and activity, thereby suppressing decay.\n\nAs a whole, the figure emphasizes that 3′ UTR structure is not simply static decoration but a regulatory layer that determines which proteins can interact with an mRNA and whether the transcript is degraded or stabilized.\n\nThe lower part extends this idea to RNA structural dynamics during development, shown for the maternal-to-zygotic transition in zebrafish. It suggests that refolding of 3′ UTRs changes which transcripts become accessible to decay machinery versus stabilizing proteins. As a result, some RNAs are degraded, whereas others are preserved, depending on their structural state and associated factors.",
